Short:        eSCL (AirScan) scanning GUI for classic AmigaOS
Author:       Darren Banfi (boingball)
Uploader:     boingball@gmail.com
Type:         gfx/misc
Version:      1.0.0
Requires:     68000+; AmigaOS 3.0+; TCP/IP stack providing bsdsocket.library
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0.0

MintSCAN
========

MintSCAN lets classic AmigaOS scan from modern network scanners and
all-in-one printers - the same eSCL/AirScan scanners phones and modern
computers already use - with no scanner-specific software needed on the
device side. It is a single GUI application, MintScan, that discovers
eSCL scanners on the LAN, queries their capabilities, and scans straight
to a file.

Sibling project to MintPRINT (IPP/AirPrint printing for classic
AmigaOS) - built the same way, reusing the same bsdsocket networking,
mDNS discovery and GadTools idioms. Scanning has no AmigaOS "device"
concept to hook into the way printing has DEVS:Printers/, so there is no
driver component here - just one GUI program.

Archive contents
----------------

This archive contains one drawer:

  MintSCAN/
    MintScan                the GUI application

Features
--------

  - LAN scanner discovery using mDNS (_uscan._tcp / _uscans._tcp)
  - eSCL capability query - reads the scanner's supported sources,
    resolutions, colour modes and document formats
  - Manual IP entry and Query, for setups mDNS multicast can't reach
    (e.g. WinUAE's SLIRP networking)
  - Scans from the flatbed or ADF, in Colour/Grayscale/Black & White,
    at 100-600 DPI, to JPEG/PNG/PDF, at A4/Letter/Legal/A3
  - Up to eight saved scanner profiles (Unit0-Unit7), switchable from
    the GUI - no separate "activate" step, whichever Unit is loaded is
    what Scan uses next
  - Scanner make/model shown after a successful query
  - A retried, timeout-bounded connection to each scanner, so a Wi-Fi
    scanner that has let its radio doze off between jobs still gets
    a fair chance to wake up and answer
  - No scanner-specific Amiga driver or vendor software required

Requirements
------------

  - Motorola 68000 or later
  - AmigaOS 3.0 or later
  - A running TCP/IP stack providing bsdsocket.library. This is
    required because MintSCAN talks directly to the scanner over IP.
  - A network scanner or all-in-one printer supporting eSCL
    (AirScan/AirPrint Scan). Most network scanners and MFPs from
    roughly the last decade support this.

MintScan performs network discovery and eSCL capability parsing and
therefore needs a larger stack than small classic Amiga utilities. Use
the supplied MintScan.info file when launching from Workbench.

Installation
------------

Extract the archive, copy the MintSCAN drawer wherever you like, and run
MintScan.

  1. Click Discover to find scanners on the LAN, or type an IP (or
     "ip:port") next to Discover and click Query.
  2. Pick a Source, Colour mode, DPI, Format and Page size.
  3. Set Save to: to where the scanned file should be written.
  4. Click Scan.
  5. Click Save Config to remember these settings in the currently
     selected Unit (Unit0 by default) for next time.

Use the Unit: dropdown to keep separate saved profiles for more than
one scanner - switching it loads that Unit's saved settings (or
MintSCAN's defaults, if that Unit has never been saved to).

AmigaOS 3.0/3.1 notes
----------------------

MintScan targets the same V39/V37-era library requirements as
MintPRINT's classic driver build, so it is expected to run unmodified
on AmigaOS 3.0/3.1 as well as later releases - it has had less
real-hardware testing on 3.0/3.1 specifically than MintPRINT has, so
reports from that combination are especially welcome.

Reporting a problem
--------------------

If Query reports that your scanner has no usable capabilities, or a
scan comes back at the wrong resolution/colour mode or won't open,
please open an issue at:

  https://github.com/boingball/MintSCAN/issues

and attach the output of windows_escl_probe.py (included in the source
repository) run against the scanner from a Windows PC on the same
network:

  python windows_escl_probe.py <scanner-ip> --dpi 400 --color BlackAndWhite1

This helps separate scanner-side eSCL behaviour from Amiga-side
problems without requiring any Amiga-specific diagnostic tools. Please
include the AmigaOS version, TCP/IP stack, and the exact
Source/Colour/DPI/Format/Size options used.

